  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Given below are two statements:</p><p>Statement I: For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi></math>, molar conductivity increases steeply with dilution.</p><p>Statement II: For carbonic acid, molar conductivity increases slowly with dilution. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "k2Hpt",
      "text": "Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "qOKAP",
      "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ajx4U",
      "text": "Statement I is true but Statement II is false",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "WD6kf",
      "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>For strong electrolytes,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;increases slowly with dilution and can be represented by the equation:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msubsup><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></math></p><p>It can be seen that if we plot&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;against&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></math>, We obtain a straight line with intercept equal to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and slope equal to &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&#39;. The value of the constant &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&#39; for a given solvent and temperature depends on the type of electrolyte.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi></math>&nbsp;is a strong electrolyte.</p><p>Weak electrolytes like carbonic&nbsp;acid and have lower degree of dissociation at higher concentrations and hence for such electrolytes, the change in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;with dilution is due to increase in the degree of dissociation and consequently the number of ions in total volume of solution that contains&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>mol</mi></math>&nbsp;of electrolyte. In such cases&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;increases steeply on dilution, especially near lower concentrations. Therefore,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msubsup></math>&nbsp;cannot be obtained by extrapolation&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mo>&and;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;to zero concentration.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/d09c56b5-375b-488c-984e-bd9b9363c7a8-image.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 149px;\" /></p></blockquote>",
